
  [1mThe fork:[0m a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter has to be supplied by the caller, and the only caller doesn't have one. Login is the
  thing that [3mestablishes[0m who the user is; before it runs, the username is all the identity that exists. So "add a userId
  parameter" can mean three different things, and they differ in what the interface looks like afterward:

  - [1mReturn a userId instead of taking one[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m keeps its [38;5;153m(username, password)[39m signature and the stub returns [38;5;153m{ [39m
    [38;5;153msuccess, user, userId }[39m. Buys you a real ID to track, costs nothing at the call site, and matches how login actually
    works. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mAdd a client-side tracking/correlation ID parameter[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, trackingId)[39m where the handler
    generates a per-attempt ID ([38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m). Buys you attempt-level tracing that ties the failed and successful
    attempts together, costs a new value the caller must mint. Useful only if you're correlating logs across a session.
  - [1mAdd [38;5;153muserId[39m literally as a third parameter[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m. The handler has nothing to pass, so
    it'd pass [38;5;153mundefined[39m or a duplicate of the username. Buys a signature that matches the words of the request, costs a
    dead parameter that every future caller has to reason about. Hardest to remove later, because it's public interface.

  [1mMy recommendation: the first.[0m The tracking goal — "track who logged in" — is about the outcome of a login, not an
  input to it, and [38;5;153musername[39m is already logged at [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m. Returning an ID gives you something stable to track by
  without putting a value at the call site that nobody can fill in.

  Worth saying plainly: the third option is what you asked for verbatim, and if you have a source for [38;5;153muserId[39m outside
  this repo that I can't see, it's the right one — I just can't wire it up without knowing where the value comes from.
